# Restaurant-Style Red Lentils with Pork Sausage

## Overview

This is a rich, hearty red lentil dish built around pork sausage, tomatoes, stock, gentle spices, and a creamy finish. It sits somewhere between a thick lentil stew, a rustic soup, and a casual restaurant-style bowl of comfort food.

The sausage gives the dish savoury depth, while the lentils break down into a thick, silky base. Coconut cream or coconut milk can be added for extra richness, but the dish also works well without it. Finish with lemon, herbs, yoghurt or crème fraîche, and good olive oil.

Serves 4–6.

## Ingredients (metric/Australian)

### Main ingredients

- 6 good pork sausages, skins removed
- 1 cup red lentils
- 1 large onion, diced
- 4 garlic cloves, sliced
- 1 carrot, finely diced
- 1 celery stalk, finely diced (optional but recommended)
- 1 tin crushed tomatoes
- 1–1.2 litres chicken stock
- 200–250 ml coconut cream OR 300–400 ml coconut milk (optional)
- 1 teaspoon ground cumin
- 1 teaspoon ground coriander
- ½ teaspoon smoked paprika
- Pinch chilli flakes
- 2 bay leaves
- Splash white wine (optional)
- Olive oil
- Knob butter

### Finish

- Juice of ½ lemon
- Parsley, chopped
- Greek yoghurt or crème fraîche
- Baby spinach or chopped spinach, stirred through at the end
- Good olive oil
- Black pepper

### To serve

- Crusty sourdough
- Olive oil
- Garlic, for rubbing or brushing onto the toast

## Oven temps and cooking temps when relevant

No oven is required.

Cook in a heavy pot over the stovetop.

- Sausage browning: medium-high to high heat
- Vegetable softening: low to medium heat
- Lentil simmer: gentle simmer
- Total cooking time: about 45–60 minutes

The lentils should be soft and partly broken down. The final texture should be thick, rich, and spoonable rather than watery.

## Instructions

1. Remove the sausage meat from the skins.

2. Heat a heavy pot with a little olive oil.

3. Brown the sausage meat hard, breaking it into rough chunks. Let some pieces get proper colour. Remove and set aside.

4. Add the onion, carrot, and celery to the pot.

5. Cook gently for 10–15 minutes until soft and sweet.

6. Add the garlic, cumin, coriander, smoked paprika, and chilli flakes.

7. Cook for about 30 seconds, stirring so the spices do not burn.

8. Deglaze with a splash of white wine if using.

9. Add the crushed tomatoes, chicken stock, red lentils, and bay leaves.

10. Simmer gently for 20–25 minutes, or until the lentils are soft and starting to break down.

11. Remove the bay leaves.

12. Blitz roughly half the lentil mixture with a stick blender, or transfer half to a blender and return it to the pot. Blend just enough to create a thick, silky base while keeping some texture.

13. Add the coconut cream or coconut milk if using.

14. Return the browned sausage meat to the pot.

15. Simmer for another 10–15 minutes. Add a little extra stock if the mixture becomes too thick.

16. Stir through a knob of butter and the lemon juice.

17. Add the spinach and stir until wilted.

18. Taste and season properly with salt and black pepper.

19. Serve in bowls with yoghurt or crème fraîche, parsley, a drizzle of good olive oil, and cracked pepper.

20. Serve with crusty sourdough, ideally toasted and brushed or rubbed with olive oil and garlic.

## Serving ideas and enhancements

- Coconut cream gives the dish a richer, rounder finish. If using it, reduce the starting stock slightly.
- Coconut milk is lighter than coconut cream and keeps the dish a little looser.
- A teaspoon of fennel seeds works very well with pork sausage.
- A tiny splash of cream at the end makes the dish richer and more restaurant-like.
- Greek yoghurt gives a fresher, lighter finish.
- Crème fraîche gives a richer, more polished finish.
- Use plenty of spinach if you have it. A whole large bag is not too much.
- Make the sourdough part of the dish rather than an afterthought. Toast it, then rub or brush with olive oil and garlic.
- If reheating leftovers, loosen with a little stock or water, as the lentils will thicken in the fridge.
